  "final_text": "**Recommendation: no, not now. Rework CRAFT first.** Both of the VP's arguments come from definition choices. When I recompute from the raw files, neither holds up.\n\n## What I recomputed (raw files match the report)\n- **CRAFT, September:** 9,162 units and €4,124,238 in list-price revenue. Source: `demand_actuals.csv` joined to `product_lines.csv` on line and model, with `week_start` in September. That is 5 week buckets (Sep 2 to Sep 30). Every row matched a price. Company total is €21,687,544 on 139,056 units, which matches the report exactly.\n- **CRAFT NCRs:** 23 records in September covering 2,832 units affected, and 24 records in August covering 2,324 units. Source: `ncr_records.csv`, filtered by `line` and month of `ncr_date`, with no duplicate IDs.\n\n## Claim 1: \"Units up 22.4% on August\"\n- **Numerator:** CRAFT units. **Denominator:** none. **Period:** September has 5 weeks and August has 4. **Source:** report §2 and `demand_actuals.csv`.\n- **Limit:** the month totals compare 5 weeks against 4 weeks.\n\n| Basis | Aug | Sep | Change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Month total (report) | 7,488 | 9,162 | **+22.4%** |\n| Per week | 1,872 | 1,832 | **−2.1%** |\n\nPer week, CRAFT demand went down slightly. The report's own §6 makes the same point about the company headline: of the +26%, only about 0.8% is real growth.\n\n## Claim 2: \"Quality is turning, 3.21 → 2.51 per 1,000 units\"\n- **Numerator:** the report counts NCR records. The alternative is units affected. **Denominator:** units shipped. **Period:** August vs September. **Source:** report §3 and `ncr_records.csv` (`ncr_id`, `qty_affected`).\n- **Limit:** NCRs track production defects, not customer returns. Units affected are production units, not shipped units, so the ratio is an intensity signal rather than a true defect rate.\n\n| CRAFT | Aug | Sep | Direction |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| NCR records per 1,000 units (report) | 3.21 | 2.51 | better |\n| NCR records per week | 6.0 | 4.6 | better |\n| **Units affected per 100 units shipped** | **31.0** | **30.9** | **flat** |\n| Units affected, total | 2,324 | 2,832 | +22% |\n| Of which scrapped | 924 | 1,539 | **+67%** |\n\nThe improvement only shows up when you count records. Measured by quantity, defects were flat at roughly 31 units affected per 100 shipped. For comparison, NEST is at about 11, PULSE about 9 and AURA about 6. Scrapped units actually rose. The VP's line \"No other line improved\" is also wrong on the quantity basis, because NEST improved from 11.8 to 10.5.\n\n## Decision\n- **Decision:** don't move more Q3 marketing budget to CRAFT yet. Put the rework first.\n- **Why:** on a per-week basis, demand is flat to slightly down (−2.1%). On a quantity basis, CRAFT's defect intensity is about 3× the next-worst line and isn't improving. More demand means more CRAFT production at roughly 31 units affected per 100 shipped.\n- **Risk if we wait:** we may give up some premium revenue for a quarter. Real margin is unknown because the data has no unit costs (§6), so the €450 average price doesn't tell us the line is profitable.\n- **Risk if we push marketing now:** more scrap and rework, and possible field failures on a €199–€699 product.\n- **The other view:** if you count NCR records and use monthly totals, the VP's case looks right (+22.4% units, defects down 3.21 → 2.51).\n- **What would change this:** CRAFT units affected falling below about 15 per 100 units shipped for two months in a row (that's roughly NEST's level, and my suggestion rather than a team standard), plus positive per-week unit growth. I've set the trigger on units affected, not record count, because the record count can drop just from merging NCRs without anything getting better.\n\n**Open point:** your team has no metric definition cards (`references/steckbriefe.md` doesn't exist). So I haven't seen an official definition of \"defect rate\" as records versus units. If your team officially counts records, the quality argument is weaker, but the per-week demand argument still stands on its own.",
